{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"agent_message","text":"The official run confirms a large gain—about 1.6–1.9× faster than the first correct kernel—but it also exposes the next bottleneck: keeping all activations and weights live together costs many vector registers. I’m testing a standard RMSNorm tradeoff now: reduce first, then immediately reread each 3 KB row from L1 for the output pass. That adds only cache-local reads while sharply lowering register pressure and can improve HBM occupancy."}}
